    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# 4.03 — Geodesics and the Exponential Map\n\nWith a connection in hand, \"straight\" has a definition that needs no surface and no ambient space:\n\n$$\\nabla_{\\dot\\gamma}\\dot\\gamma = 0 \\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad \\ddot x^k + \\Gamma^k_{ij}\\dot x^i\\dot x^j = 0 .$$\n\n**A geodesic parallel-transports its own velocity.** It does not turn, because the only notion of turning available is measured by $\\nabla$, and $\\nabla$ says the velocity is unchanging.\n\nThese are the same equations as 3.07 — but there they were derived from a surface sitting in $\\mathbb R^3$, by demanding that acceleration have no tangential component. Here there is no \"tangential\" and no \"normal\"; the equation comes straight from the connection.\n\n**Straightest = shortest.** 3.07 asserted the equivalence and deferred the proof. It follows from the calculus of variations: the Euler–Lagrange equations for the energy functional\n\n$$E(\\gamma) = \\tfrac12\\int g_{ij}\\dot x^i\\dot x^j\\,dt$$\n\nare exactly the geodesic equations. We derive them below and check the two agree. (Energy rather than length, because length is reparametrisation-invariant and so has a degenerate variational problem; minimising energy picks out the constant-speed parametrisation as a bonus.)\n\n**The exponential map.** Geodesics through a point $p$ are determined by their initial velocity, so\n\n$$\\exp_p : T_pM \\to M, \\qquad \\exp_p(X) = \\gamma_X(1)$$\n\nsends a tangent vector to the point you reach by travelling along the geodesic it points down. Using it as a chart gives **normal coordinates**, in which\n\n$$g_{ij}(p) = \\delta_{ij}, \\qquad \\Gamma^k_{ij}(p) = 0 .$$\n\nEvery manifold is flat to first order at every point. Curvature is a *second*-order phenomenon — which is why $\\Gamma$, being first-order data, can be transformed away, while the curvature tensor of 4.04 cannot.\n\nIn relativity that statement is the **equivalence principle**: a freely falling observer sees no gravitational field locally, and detects gravity only through tidal effects, which are second-order.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nload(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\nts(e) := trigsimp(e)$\n\nchristoffel(g, vars) := block([ginv, n_],\n  n_ : length(vars),  ginv : ratsimp(invert(g)),\n  makelist( makelist( makelist(\n    ts( sum( ginv[k][l]*( diff(g[j][l], vars[i])\n                        + diff(g[i][l], vars[j])\n                        - diff(g[i][j], vars[l]) ), l, 1, n_)/2 ),\n    j,1,n_), i,1,n_), k,1,n_) )$\n\nassume(y > 0, u > 0, u < %pi)$\n\n/* --- Straightest: ∇_γ' γ' = 0 --- */\ngeo_eqs(g, vars) := block([G, n_], G : christoffel(g, vars), n_ : length(vars),\n  makelist( 'diff(vars[k], t, 2)\n            + sum( sum( G[k][i][j]*'diff(vars[i],t)*'diff(vars[j],t), j,1,n_),\n                   i,1,n_), k, 1, n_) )$\n\ng_hyp : matrix([1/y^2, 0], [0, 1/y^2])$\nGE : geo_eqs(g_hyp, [x,y])$\nprint(\"hyperbolic half-plane, g = \", g_hyp)$\nprint(\"geodesic equations from ∇_γ'γ' = 0:\")$\nfor e in GE do print(\"   \", e, \" = 0\")$\n\n/* --- Shortest: Euler–Lagrange for the energy functional --- */\ndepends([x, y], t)$\nLag : ( diff(x,t)^2 + diff(y,t)^2 ) / (2*y^2)$        /* ½ g_ij ẋⁱẋʲ */\nEL(L_, q) := ratsimp( diff( diff(L_, diff(q,t)), t ) - diff(L_, q) )$\nELx : EL(Lag, x)$   ELy : EL(Lag, y)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Euler–Lagrange for E = ½∫(ẋ² + ẏ²)/y² dt:\")$\nprint(\"   in x : \", ELx)$\nprint(\"   in y : \", ELy)$\n\n/* Each E–L equation is 1/y² times the corresponding geodesic equation. */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"multiplying by y²:\")$\nprint(\"   y²·(E–L in x) = \", ratsimp( ELx*y^2 ))$\nprint(\"   y²·(E–L in y) = \", ratsimp( ELy*y^2 ))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"differences against the geodesic equations:\")$\nprint(\"   \", ratsimp( ELx*y^2 - GE[1] ))$\nprint(\"   \", ratsimp( ELy*y^2 - GE[2] ))$\nprint(\"   both zero ⟹ straightest and shortest are the same equations ∎\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"(The factor y² is exactly g₁₁⁻¹: E–L produces the equations with an\")$\nprint(\" index lowered, and raising it with g^{ij} recovers the geodesic form.)\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 2,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "hyperbolic half-plane, g =  matrix([1/y^2,0],[0,1/y^2])\ngeodesic equations from ∇_γ'γ' = 0:\n    'diff(x,t,2)-(2*'diff(x,t,1)*'diff(y,t,1))/y  = 0\n    'diff(y,t,2)-('diff(y,t,1))^2/y+('diff(x,t,1))^2/y  = 0\nEuler–Lagrange for E = ½∫(ẋ² + ẏ²)/y² dt:\n   in x :  -((2*'diff(x,t,1)*'diff(y,t,1)-'diff(x,t,2)*y)/y^3)\n   in y :  (y*'diff(y,t,2)-('diff(y,t,1))^2+('diff(x,t,1))^2)/y^3\nmultiplying by y²:\n   y²·(E–L in x) =  -((2*'diff(x,t,1)*'diff(y,t,1)-'diff(x,t,2)*y)/y)\n   y²·(E–L in y) =  (y*'diff(y,t,2)-('diff(y,t,1))^2+('diff(x,t,1))^2)/y\ndifferences against the geodesic equations:\n    0\n    0\n   both zero ⟹ straightest and shortest are the same equations ∎\n(The factor y² is exactly g₁₁⁻¹: E–L produces the equations with an\n index lowered, and raising it with g^{ij} recovers the geodesic form.)"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* --- Solving the hyperbolic geodesic equations --- */\nremove([x,y], dependency)$\nchk_geo(xf, yf, label) := block([r1, r2],\n  r1 : ratsimp( diff(xf,t,2) - 2*diff(xf,t)*diff(yf,t)/yf ),\n  r2 : ratsimp( diff(yf,t,2) - diff(yf,t)^2/yf + diff(xf,t)^2/yf ),\n  print(\"   \", label),\n  print(\"      residuals: \", ts(r1), \" , \", ts(r2),\n        if ts(r1)=0 and ts(r2)=0 then \"   GEODESIC ✓\" else \"   no\") )$\n\nprint(\"candidate geodesics on the hyperbolic half-plane:\")$\nchk_geo( 0, exp(t),                       \"vertical line x = 0, y = e^t\" )$\nchk_geo( rr*tanh(t), rr*sech(t),          \"semicircle of radius r centred on the axis\" )$\nchk_geo( 3 + rr*tanh(t), rr*sech(t),      \"the same, centred at x = 3\" )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Vertical lines and semicircles orthogonal to the real axis —\")$\nprint(\"exactly the geodesics 2.06 obtained from the Möbius group.\")$\nprint(\"There they came from a symmetry argument; here from an ODE.\")$\n\n/* --- Normal coordinates: flat to first order --- */\n/* The sphere near the north pole, as a graph z = √(1 − x² − y²). */\nf_ : sqrt(1 - x^2 - y^2)$\ng_np : matrix([1 + diff(f_,x)^2, diff(f_,x)*diff(f_,y)],\n              [diff(f_,x)*diff(f_,y), 1 + diff(f_,y)^2])$\ng_np : ratsimp(g_np)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"unit sphere near the pole, graph chart:  g = \", g_np)$\nprint(\"   g at the origin      = \", subst([x=0,y=0], g_np), \"   = δ_ij ✓\")$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"first derivatives of g at the origin:\")$\nfor i : 1 thru 2 do for j : 1 thru 2 do\n  print(\"   ∂g\", i, j, \"/∂x = \", subst([x=0,y=0], diff(g_np[i][j], x)),\n        \"     ∂g\", i, j, \"/∂y = \", subst([x=0,y=0], diff(g_np[i][j], y)))$\n\nGnp : christoffel(g_np, [x,y])$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   Γ at the origin: Γ¹ = \", subst([x=0,y=0], Gnp[1]),\n      \"   Γ² = \", subst([x=0,y=0], Gnp[2]))$\nprint(\"   all zero — the metric is flat TO FIRST ORDER at this point.\")$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"second derivatives do not vanish:\")$\nprint(\"   ∂²g₁₁/∂x² at 0 = \", subst([x=0,y=0], diff(g_np[1][1], x, 2)))$\nprint(\"   ∂²g₂₂/∂y² at 0 = \", subst([x=0,y=0], diff(g_np[2][2], y, 2)))$\nprint(\"   and that second-order data is the curvature (4.04).\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"This is why Γ can be transformed away at a point but curvature cannot,\")$\nprint(\"and it is the equivalence principle: free fall abolishes gravity\")$\nprint(\"locally, leaving only tidal effects.\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 3,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "candidate geodesics on the hyperbolic half-plane:\n    vertical line x = 0, y = e^t\n      residuals:  0  ,  0    GEODESIC ✓\n    semicircle of radius r centred on the axis\n      residuals:  0  ,  0    GEODESIC ✓\n    the same, centred at x = 3\n      residuals:  0  ,  0    GEODESIC ✓\nVertical lines and semicircles orthogonal to the real axis —\nexactly the geodesics 2.06 obtained from the Möbius group.\nThere they came from a symmetry argument; here from an ODE.\nunit sphere near the pole, graph chart:  g =\n matrix([(y^2-1)/(y^2+x^2-1),-((x*y)/(y^2+x^2-1))],\n        [-((x*y)/(y^2+x^2-1)),(x^2-1)/(y^2+x^2-1)])\n   g at the origin      =  matrix([1,0],[0,1])    = δ_ij ✓\nfirst derivatives of g at the origin:\n   ∂g 1 1 /∂x =  0      ∂g 1 1 /∂y =  0\n   ∂g 1 2 /∂x =  0      ∂g 1 2 /∂y =  0\n   ∂g 2 1 /∂x =  0      ∂g 2 1 /∂y =  0\n   ∂g 2 2 /∂x =  0      ∂g 2 2 /∂y =  0\n   Γ at the origin: Γ¹ =  [[0,0],[0,0]]    Γ² =  [[0,0],[0,0]]\n   all zero — the metric is flat TO FIRST ORDER at this point.\nsecond derivatives do not vanish:\n   ∂²g₁₁/∂x² at 0 =  2\n   ∂²g₂₂/∂y² at 0 =  2\n   and that second-order data is the curvature (4.04).\nThis is why Γ can be transformed away at a point but curvature cannot,\nand it is the equivalence principle: free fall abolishes gravity\nlocally, leaving only tidal effects."
