      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Summary\n\n$$\\nabla_{\\dot\\gamma}\\dot\\gamma = 0 \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\ddot x^k + \\Gamma^k_{ij}\\dot x^i\\dot x^j = 0 \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\delta\\!\\int \\tfrac12 g_{ij}\\dot x^i\\dot x^j\\,dt = 0$$\n\n**Straightest = shortest, proved.** 3.07 asserted the equivalence; here the Euler–Lagrange equations for the energy functional were computed and found to be exactly $g^{ij}$ times the geodesic equations — differences identically zero. (An earlier attempt used the wrong factor and the residuals came out non-zero, which is how the slip was caught: the correct factor is $g_{11}^{-1} = y^2$, not $y^2/2$.)\n\n**The hyperbolic geodesics come back.** Vertical lines and semicircles orthogonal to the real axis satisfy the equations identically — the same curves 2.06 obtained from Möbius invariance. Two entirely different derivations, one answer:\n\n| Route | Notebook |\n|---|---|\n| symmetry: $PSL_2(\\mathbb R)$ preserves circles $\\perp \\mathbb R$ | 2.06 |\n| ODE: $\\ddot x^k + \\Gamma^k_{ij}\\dot x^i\\dot x^j = 0$ | here |\n\n**Normal coordinates, verified.** In the graph chart at the pole of the unit sphere, $g(0) = \\delta_{ij}$, **all eight first derivatives vanish**, and hence $\\Gamma(0) = 0$. But $\\partial^2 g_{11}/\\partial x^2 = 2 \\ne 0$: the second-order data survives. That is the whole reason curvature is a tensor while $\\Gamma$ is not — first-order data can be transformed away at a point, second-order data cannot.\n\nPhysically this is the **equivalence principle**. A freely falling observer can always choose coordinates in which the connection vanishes at their location, so gravity disappears locally; what they cannot remove are the second derivatives, which appear as **tidal forces**. 6.02 computes exactly those for the Schwarzschild metric.\n\n## Next\n\n**4.04 — The Curvature Tensor** builds the object that cannot be transformed away. It measures the failure of covariant derivatives to commute, $R(X,Y)Z = \\nabla_X\\nabla_Y Z - \\nabla_Y\\nabla_X Z - \\nabla_{[X,Y]}Z$, and reproduces the holonomy of 4.02 in infinitesimal form.\n\n## References\n\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2018) *Introduction to Riemannian Manifolds*. Cham: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9. Chapter 4 (geodesics), Chapter 5 (exponential map, normal coordinates), Chapter 6 (geodesics as minimisers, and the precise sense in which they are only *locally* minimising).\n- do Carmo, M.P. (1992) *Riemannian Geometry*. Boston: Birkhäuser. Chapter 3.\n- ★ Lee, T., Leok, M. and McClamroch, N.H. (2018) *Global Formulations of Lagrangian and Hamiltonian Dynamics on Manifolds*. Cham: Springer. The variational route in mechanical language, where the energy functional is literally an action.\n- ★ Callahan, J.J. (2000) *The Geometry of Spacetime*. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-6736-0. Chapter 6, for normal coordinates and the equivalence principle together.\n\n**A caveat on \"shortest\".** Geodesics are only *locally* length-minimising. On the sphere, a great-circle arc longer than half the circumference is a geodesic but not the shortest path between its endpoints — the other way round is shorter. Where minimisation fails is governed by conjugate points, which 4.06 treats.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = in the local indexed library (`maths` collection); the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
      "metadata": {}
